SQL JOINS, SUBQUERIES, AGGREGATE FUNCTIONS AND INDEXES

1. Objective

To understand SQL Joins, Subqueries, Aggregate Functions and Indexes and perform basic reporting queries using MySQL.

2. SQL JOINS

JOIN is used to combine data from two or more tables using a common column.

INNER JOIN

INNER JOIN returns only matching records from both tables.

SELECT employees.name, departments.dep_name
FROM employees
INNER JOIN departments
ON employees.dep_id = departments.dep_id;

LEFT JOIN

LEFT JOIN returns all records from the left table and matching records from the right table.

SELECT employees.name, departments.dep_name
FROM employees
LEFT JOIN departments
ON employees.dep_id = departments.dep_id;

RIGHT JOIN

RIGHT JOIN returns all records from the right table and matching records from the left table.

SELECT employees.name, departments.dep_name
FROM employees
RIGHT JOIN departments
ON employees.dep_id = departments.dep_id;

3. SUBQUERIES

A Subquery is a query written inside another SQL query.

Example: Find the employee having the highest salary.

SELECT *
FROM employees
WHERE salary = (
    SELECT MAX(salary)
    FROM employees
);

4. AGGREGATE FUNCTIONS

Aggregate functions perform calculations on multiple rows and return a single result.

COUNT() – Counts total records.

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM employees;

SUM() – Calculates total salary.

SELECT SUM(salary) FROM employees;

AVG() – Calculates average salary.

SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees;

MAX() – Finds maximum salary.

SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees;

MIN() – Finds minimum salary.

SELECT MIN(salary) FROM employees;

5. INDEXES

An Index is used to make searching records faster in a database.

Example: Create an index on the employee name column.

CREATE INDEX idx_employee_name
ON employees(name);

Delete the index.

DROP INDEX idx_employee_name
ON employees;

6. PRACTICAL REPORTING QUERIES

Employee + Department

Display employee names with their department names.

SELECT employees.name, departments.dep_name
FROM employees
INNER JOIN departments
ON employees.dep_id = departments.dep_id;

Employee + Salary

Display employee names with their salaries.

SELECT name, salary
FROM employees;

Department-wise Salary

Display total salary of each department.

SELECT dep_id, SUM(salary) AS total_salary
FROM employees
GROUP BY dep_id;

Highest Paid Employee

Find the employee who receives the highest salary.

SELECT *
FROM employees
WHERE salary = (
    SELECT MAX(salary)
    FROM employees
);

7. Conclusion

In this practical, we learned SQL Joins, Subqueries, Aggregate Functions and Indexes. We also created basic reporting queries to display employee details, department information and salary records.